<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fort Dansborg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1620">1620</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fort Dansborg is a Danish fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharangambadi">Tranquebar</a>, now Tharangambadi, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, built in 1620 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ove-gjedde">Ove Gjedde</a> on land granted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayak</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a>. It was the seat of the Danish governors and the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>'s Asian trade for over two centuries, until Tranquebar was sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1845. It now houses a museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fort-dansborg.jpg" alt="Fort Dansborg" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Esben Agersnap · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_Dansborg.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Fort Dansborg stands on the shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> in the southern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharangambadi">Tharangambadi</a>, in the Mayiladuthurai District, 283 km from the state capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>. It is the second largest Danish fort after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kronborg">Kronborg</a>. Built of brick in Danish style, with large halls, columned structures and high ceilings, it is trapezoidal, about 60 m long on the side facing the sea and about 11 m wide. The governor's residence, a kitchen and a church room lay in its wings and centre, and four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a>-hump domes rest on a central pillar.</p><p>The basement served as store room, prison and soldiers' rest room, while the governor and priests lived on the upper level. The citadel around it held the Masilamaninathar Temple of the 13th century, the Zion Church of 1701, the New Jerusalem Church of 1718, the governor's bungalow of 1784 and the town gateway of 1792, laid out like a small European town along King's Street.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danish-east-india-company">Danish East India Company</a> was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> in 1616 and sent a mission under Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ove-gjedde">Ove Gjedde</a>, who in November 1620, despite Portuguese resistance, signed a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raghunatha-nayak">Raghunatha Nayak</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a>. It ceded an area of 8 km by 4 km for a yearly rent and let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> collect taxes from neighbouring villages; the treaty, on a golden leaf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, is kept in the Danish royal archives. A factory set up after the fort minted coins marked TB or DB, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> textiles were the colony's main export.</p><p>Two Protestant missionaries sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> by King Frederick IV, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartholomaus-ziegenbalg">Bartholomäus Ziegenbalg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-plutschau">Heinrich Plütschau</a>, arrived on 9 July 1706, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tranquebar-mission">Tranquebar Mission</a> and printed the first Tamil New Testament on a press inside the fort. In the middle of the 18th century the town's commercial importance declined as textile production moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serampore">Serampore</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharangambadi">Tranquebar</a> remained the colony's headquarters until the sale of 1845.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s independence the state government used the fort as an inspection bungalow until 1978, when the Tamil Nadu State Department of Archaeology took it over; it is now a museum of the fort and the Danish colonial empire in India. The Tranquebar Association, formed in 2001 with the Danish royal family, restored its south end by 2005, and a stone shore defence against erosion was laid in 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fort Dansborg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Danes built <b>Fort Dansborg</b> there, which became the centre of their Asian trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/danish-india">Danish India</a></li></ul></section>
